From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenSec <contactus@opensec.in>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 09:10:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fetch: an idle connection waits in the keep-alive pool for 4
 seconds, or as long as the server says

A connection that a response had come back on was kept for 5 minutes, and
used for the next request to the same host. One that has sat idle may be gone
without a FIN or RST having come back (a NAT or a load balancer that forgot
it, a network that went away while the machine slept), and a request written
to it waits for an answer until it times out. Node closes an idle connection
after 4 seconds, so programs written for it (Pi's model providers, its
extensions) never met this.

Now, as undici does it: 4 seconds, or what the response's `Keep-Alive:
timeout=N` says less 2 seconds (at most 600); a fetch session's own idle
timeout stands. BUN_CONFIG_HTTP_KEEPALIVE_TIMEOUT sets the default. And the
pool does not hand out a socket that has been parked for longer than that by
the wall clock: the idle timer's ticks are seconds apart and stand still while
the machine sleeps. Preconnected sockets and HTTP/2 sessions keep 5 minutes.
